# The families (141 + godfather)
{141}{}{We have nobody's blessing. That can't last.}
{142}{}{Mordino is our godfather. Big Jesus takes a quarter and treats our back door as his own.}
{143}{}{Wright is our godfather. Cheap whiskey, no chems in the house, and Orville watches everything.}
{144}{}{Salvatore is our godfather. His men watch the door. He takes thirty percent and never says what for.}
{145}{}{Bishop is our godfather. He takes his fifth and remembers our name in the NCR.}
{146}{}{We have no godfather. We pay nobody, and nobody protects us. Every family in town counts that as an insult.}
{147}{}{Not everybody is pleased.}
{148}{}{Nobody holds a grudge right now. That won't last either.}
{151}{}{The Mordinos are counting.}
{152}{}{The Wrights are watching.}
{153}{}{Salvatore has stopped smiling at us.}
{154}{}{Bishop asks about us too often.}

# Next meeting
{160}{}{No family seat, no family table. Build the top floor into a seat worth sitting at, and the families will come to us.}
{161}{}{They're waiting now. Every hour you make them wait is an insult.}
{162}{}{In }
{163}{}{ week(s). I'll send word.}

# Meeting: Salvatore wants more
{210}{}{Salvatore's man was here. The price of friendship went up: ten percent more, starting now. He said "or", and then he didn't finish the sentence.}
{211}{}{Pay him.}
{212}{}{[Speech] Remind him how much his tables make on our customers.}
{213}{}{No. Not one cent more.}
{214}{}{Paid. Salvatore's man smiled. That's the most expensive smile in New Reno.}
{215}{}{(Asch writes something and underlines it.) He listened. Then he left the price where it was. You made him look reasonable, and he'll remember that you could.}
{216}{}{(Asch closes the ledger.) Then it's war, the quiet kind. Last night somebody broke every bottle behind our bar. Three hundred dollars. It won't be the last bill.}

# Meeting: the Mordinos' Jet deal
{220}{}{The Mordinos want to sell through our back door. Three hundred a week for us, clean. For everybody else, Jet.}
{221}{}{Take the deal.}
{222}{}{No Jet in my house.}
{223}{}{Done. The first crates came in before midnight. Roz won't look at me. The Wrights won't look at anybody.}
{224}{}{Big Jesus laughed when he heard. That's worse than when he shouts.}

# Meeting: Wright wants a name
{230}{}{Orville Wright wants to know who sold the Jet that killed his son. He thinks our girls hear things. He's right. They do.}
{231}{}{Tell him what we know.}
{232}{}{We don't hear anything. We're a brothel, not a confessional.}
{233}{}{He got a name. By morning the man was hanging from the water tower. The Wrights will remember who gave them the name, and so will the Mordinos.}
{234}{}{Orville said nothing. He left his hat on the table and walked out. Orville never forgets his hat.}

# Meeting: Bishop's Shark Club
{240}{}{Bishop offers us a share in the Shark Club. A thousand now, a hundred every week, for as long as he's alive.}
{241}{}{Pay the thousand.}
{242}{}{Remind him what was in his letter to Westin.}
{243}{}{We're not interested.}
{244}{}{Paid. Bishop sent a bottle and a note: "Partners." He's never had a partner who lived long.}
{245}{}{(Asch goes pale for a moment.) He gave us the share. He also looked at me the way you look at a man you'll have to replace. Be careful.}
{246}{}{Bishop doesn't take no personally. He takes it professionally.}

# Meeting: a runner is skimming
{250}{}{One of the runners is skimming. Twenty, thirty a week. Not enough to kill him for. Enough to notice.}
{251}{}{[Perception] Show me the ledgers. I'll find him.}
{252}{}{[Speech] Bring him in. I'll talk to him.}
{253}{}{Make an example of him.}
{254}{}{It takes you ten minutes: the same runner, always a few dollars short on the Redding route. He gives back two hundred. He'll never be short again.}
{255}{}{(The runner cries. Then he talks.) He owed Salvatore. Now he owes you, and he knows it. He'll run twice as fast.}
{256}{}{They found him in the morning behind the Stables. Every runner in New Reno heard about it by noon. None of them will ever be short again.}

# Look
{300}{}{An old man with ink-stained fingers and a ledger he never closes.}
{301}{}{Leopold Asch, consigliere. He has kept the books for three families and outlived two of them.}
